From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 00:59:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/28] flashnext: CUDA copy windows to 64 rows

A lone stream's verify window held 32 rows, so a copied continuation
stopped at 31 drafts. It now grows to 63 after a copy that lands whole,
through the core's existing widening rule, and falls back after one
that breaks. The MTP chain keeps its 15 drafts (TF_FLASHNEXT_DRAFTS 1
to 15), streams that share a round keep their 16-row windows, and a
shared verify pass keeps its 64 rows, so the row counts past the
kernels' 64-row switch are unchanged: a 64-row lone window runs the
same kernels at a row count a shared pass already serves.

Every shape the window widens derives from mtp.max_rows: the draft's
scores and best buffers, the saved DeltaNet rows, the KV pool's
window_rows and the sequence capacity. The per-drafting-stream device
delta is 32 more rows of scores (15.2 MiB at vocab 248,320), 36.8 MiB
of saved rows and 12.8 KB of best values, 52.1 MiB all together, held once per live drafting
stream; the shared verify's scores scratch already held round_rows.

A load now probes the copied continuations' row invariance on the real
weights before anything serves: every row of every width 1..64 must
score byte-equal to its one-token step, the 32- and 64-row paths
overlapping wherever a window is common to both. A differing row falls
back to the upstream 32-row windows for the load; a probe that cannot
run says so and keeps 64. TF_FLASHNEXT_WINDOW_CHECK=0 skips the probe.
